Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and why they matter  

orders  
• id : links the sales order we must fulfil (id = 423).  

orderlines  
• order_id : joins to orders.  
• id : natural sequence of the order lines (helps with “sequence and cumulative quantities”).  
• product_id, qty : what and how much has been ordered.  

inventory  
• id : internal reference to each inventory batch.  
• product_id, qty : what and how much is stored in one location.  
• location_id : where the batch is stored.  
• purchase_id : lets us find the purchase date so that we can “prioritise earlier purchased dates”.  

purchases  
• id : joins to inventory.purchase_id.  
• purchased : the incoming‑stock date used for FIFO picking.  

locations  
• id : joins to inventory.location_id.  
• warehouse : filter to warehouse = 1.  
• aisle, position : physical co‑ordinates we must present in the picking list.  

Conditions that will be used  
• orders.id = 423  
• locations.warehouse = 1  
• inventory.product_id IN (SELECT product_id FROM orderlines WHERE order_id = 423)  
• inventory.qty > 0  
• Pick batches ordered by purchases.purchased ASC, inventory.qty ASC  
• For products that appear on several order lines, respect the cumulative quantity of all those lines.  

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Exploratory → final SQL (10 different SELECT statements)

```sql
-- 1. Inspect the order lines that belong to order 423
SELECT id AS orderline_id,
       product_id,
       qty
FROM   orderlines
WHERE  order_id = 423
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. How many distinct products are on that order?
SELECT DISTINCT product_id
FROM   orderlines
WHERE  order_id = 423;
```

```sql
-- 3. See the inventory that exists for those products (all warehouses)
SELECT id          AS inventory_id,
       product_id,
       qty,
       location_id,
       purchase_id
FROM   inventory
WHERE  product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                      FROM   orderlines
                      WHERE  order_id = 423)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Add physical co‑ordinates and warehouse so we can filter to warehouse 1
SELECT i.id        AS inventory_id,
       i.product_id,
       i.qty        AS inv_qty,
       l.warehouse,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM   inventory  AS i
JOIN   locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
WHERE  i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                        FROM   orderlines
                        WHERE  order_id = 423)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Restrict to warehouse 1 only
SELECT i.id        AS inventory_id,
       i.product_id,
       i.qty,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM   inventory  AS i
JOIN   locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
WHERE  l.warehouse = 1
  AND  i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                        FROM   orderlines
                        WHERE  order_id = 423)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Bring in the purchase date to apply FIFO
SELECT i.id        AS inventory_id,
       i.product_id,
       i.qty        AS inv_qty,
       p.purchased,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM   inventory  AS i
JOIN   purchases  AS p ON p.id = i.purchase_id
JOIN   locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
WHERE  l.warehouse = 1
  AND  i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                        FROM   orderlines
                        WHERE  order_id = 423)
ORDER BY i.product_id,
         date(p.purchased),
         i.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Calculate the total quantity needed per product for order 423
SELECT product_id,
       SUM(qty) AS total_needed
FROM   orderlines
WHERE  order_id = 423
GROUP  BY product_id;
```

```sql
-- 8. Show inventory with a running (cumulative) sum to see where the demand will be covered
WITH inv AS (
   SELECT i.product_id,
          i.qty          AS inv_qty,
          p.purchased,
          l.aisle,
          l.position
   FROM   inventory  AS i
   JOIN   purchases  AS p ON p.id = i.purchase_id
   JOIN   locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
   WHERE  l.warehouse = 1
     AND  i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                           FROM   orderlines
                           WHERE  order_id = 423)
)
SELECT product_id,
       inv_qty,
       purchased,
       aisle,
       position,
       SUM(inv_qty) OVER (PARTITION BY product_id
                          ORDER BY date(purchased), inv_qty) AS cum_inv
FROM   inv
ORDER BY product_id,
         date(purchased),
         inv_qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Prepare table of required qty (need) and inventory batches (inv) for allocation
WITH need AS (
  SELECT product_id,
         SUM(qty) AS need_qty
  FROM   orderlines
  WHERE  order_id = 423
  GROUP  BY product_id
),
inv AS (
  SELECT i.id          AS inventory_id,
         i.product_id,
         i.qty          AS inv_qty,
         p.purchased,
         l.aisle,
         l.position
  FROM   inventory  AS i
  JOIN   purchases  AS p ON p.id = i.purchase_id
  JOIN   locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
  WHERE  l.warehouse = 1
    AND  i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                          FROM   orderlines
                          WHERE  order_id = 423)
)
SELECT inv.*,
       need.need_qty
FROM   inv
JOIN   need ON need.product_id = inv.product_id
ORDER BY inv.product_id,
         date(inv.purchased),
         inv.inv_qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final picking proposal:
--    * FIFO within each product (earlier purchase date, then smaller batch)
--    * Stop when cumulative picked = demand
WITH order_products AS (
  SELECT product_id,
         SUM(qty) AS need_qty
  FROM   orderlines
  WHERE  order_id = 423
  GROUP  BY product_id
),
inv AS (
  SELECT i.product_id,
         i.qty          AS inv_qty,
         p.purchased,
         l.aisle,
         l.position
  FROM   inventory  AS i
  JOIN   purchases  AS p ON p.id = i.purchase_id
  JOIN   locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
  WHERE  l.warehouse = 1
    AND  i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id
                          FROM   orderlines
                          WHERE  order_id = 423)
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT inv.*,
         SUM(inv_qty)
           OVER (PARTITION BY product_id
                 ORDER BY date(purchased), inv_qty) AS cum_inv
  FROM   inv
),
picks AS (
  SELECT r.product_id,
         r.aisle,
         r.position,
         CASE
           WHEN r.cum_inv - r.inv_qty >= op.need_qty THEN 0                            -- demand already covered
           WHEN r.cum_inv >= op.need_qty          THEN op.need_qty - (r.cum_inv - r.inv_qty) -- take only what is still missing
           ELSE r.inv_qty                                                             -- take whole batch
         END AS pick_qty
  FROM   ranked r
  JOIN   order_products op ON op.product_id = r.product_id
)
SELECT product_id,
       aisle,
       position,
       pick_qty
FROM   picks
WHERE  pick_qty > 0
ORDER  BY product_id,
         aisle,
         position
LIMIT 20;
```

Each successive query deepens the insight, culminating in a single statement (Query 10) that produces the exact product‑/aisle‑/position‑/quantity list required to pick order 423 in warehouse 1, adhering to FIFO and batch‑size priorities while matching the cumulative ordered quantities.
